What is Horseradish Peroxidase (HRP)?
Horseradish peroxidase (HRP) is an enzyme derived from the roots of the horseradish plant (Armoracia rusticana). It catalyzes the oxidation of various organic and inorganic substrates using hydrogen peroxide as an electron acceptor. This reaction produces a color change or light emission, making HRP invaluable in molecular biology and clinical diagnostics.
The global horseradish peroxidase (HRP) market is experiencing steady growth driven by the increasing demand for enzyme-based diagnostics and research applications. HRP, a key enzyme used in immunoassays such as ELISA and western blotting, plays a crucial role in biomedical research, diagnostics, and pharmaceutical manufacturing. The market is benefiting from the expansion of the biotechnology and pharmaceutical industries, along with the growing prevalence of chronic and infectious diseases that require accurate and efficient diagnostic tools. Additionally, advancements in enzyme purification and recombinant HRP production technologies are enhancing product efficiency and scalability, further fueling market growth. However, the market also faces challenges such as batch-to-batch variability in natural HRP sources and high production costs. North America and Europe dominate the market due to their well-established research infrastructure, while Asia-Pacific is expected to witness the fastest growth due to increased investment in healthcare and biotech sectors.
